WEBVTT

00:00:00.105 --> 00:00:02.342
Two transactions run at the same time.

00:00:02.763 --> 00:00:04.684
When is their interleaving still correct?

00:00:05.118 --> 00:00:07.092
The course gives two answers:

00:00:07.553 --> 00:00:09.303
view-serializability and

00:00:09.316 --> 00:00:10.961
conflict-serializability.

00:00:11.395 --> 00:00:13.447
They are not the same thing, and the

00:00:13.447 --> 00:00:15.618
difference is a classic exam trap.

00:00:16.437 --> 00:00:18.897
A schedule is serializable if it leaves

00:00:18.897 --> 00:00:21.384
the database in the same state as some

00:00:21.384 --> 00:00:24.055
serial schedule of the same transactions.

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:26.397
To check that, we need a notion of

00:00:26.411 --> 00:00:29.148
equivalence, and each notion gives a

00:00:29.161 --> 00:00:30.700
different class of schedules.

00:00:31.549 --> 00:00:34.418
View-equivalence asks for three things:

00:00:34.852 --> 00:00:37.641
the same operations, every read reads from

00:00:37.641 --> 00:00:40.102
the same write, and every object gets its

00:00:40.115 --> 00:00:41.931
final value from the same write.

00:00:42.339 --> 00:00:44.365
A schedule that is view-equivalent to a

00:00:44.365 --> 00:00:46.483
serial one is in VSR.

00:00:47.331 --> 00:00:50.042
Conflict-serializability is stricter.

00:00:50.476 --> 00:00:53.134
Two operations conflict when they belong

00:00:53.134 --> 00:00:55.594
to different transactions, touch the same

00:00:55.608 --> 00:00:58.108
object, and at least one of them is a

00:00:58.121 --> 00:00:58.581
write.

00:00:59.015 --> 00:01:00.568
Two reads never conflict.

00:01:01.409 --> 00:01:03.146
Take this schedule from the slides.

00:01:03.567 --> 00:01:08.409
T1 reads x, T2 writes x, T1 writes x, and

00:01:08.422 --> 00:01:09.909
T3 writes x.

00:01:10.225 --> 00:01:12.936
T2 and T1 write x without reading it

00:01:12.949 --> 00:01:13.462
first:

00:01:13.765 --> 00:01:14.909
these are blind writes.

00:01:15.751 --> 00:01:17.804
Now build the conflict graph:

00:01:18.093 --> 00:01:21.080
one node per transaction, one arc for

00:01:21.264 --> 00:01:23.620
every conflicting pair, in order.

00:01:24.041 --> 00:01:28.330
r1 before w2 gives an arc from T1 to T2,

00:01:28.659 --> 00:01:32.172
and w2 before w1 gives an arc from T2 back

00:01:32.185 --> 00:01:32.804
to T1.

00:01:33.251 --> 00:01:35.988
That is a cycle, so the schedule is not

00:01:35.988 --> 00:01:37.409
conflict-serializable.

00:01:38.229 --> 00:01:39.900
Yet compare it with the serial schedule

00:01:39.913 --> 00:01:42.598
T1, T2, T3.

00:01:43.006 --> 00:01:46.308
T1 still reads the initial value of x, and

00:01:46.321 --> 00:01:48.992
T3 still writes the final value.

00:01:49.413 --> 00:01:52.071
Same reads-from, same final write:

00:01:52.624 --> 00:01:54.756
the schedule is view-serializable.

00:01:55.203 --> 00:01:56.742
The blind writes in the middle are

00:01:56.848 --> 00:01:59.150
overwritten, so their order does not

00:01:59.163 --> 00:02:00.163
matter to anyone.

00:02:01.019 --> 00:02:03.861
So every conflict-serializable schedule is

00:02:03.875 --> 00:02:06.559
view-serializable, but not the other way

00:02:06.559 --> 00:02:07.059
around.

00:02:07.467 --> 00:02:09.506
A cycle in the conflict graph rules out

00:02:09.519 --> 00:02:11.809
CSR, not VSR.

00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:15.638
Deciding VSR is much harder, which is why

00:02:15.651 --> 00:02:18.361
real schedulers rely on the conflict test.
